Contamination and calibration errors are common causes of a wrong result: dirty glassware, an uncalibrated balance, a reagent bottle left open, a bench not cleaned.

Other frequent errors are:
recording data on loose paper or after the practical, then copying it out;
rounding too early in a calculation, or mixing units;
using an expired reagent or a badly stored standard;
reporting a result without its unit, its uncertainty or the method used;
skipping quality control because the sample seemed simple;
mouth pipetting or eating in the laboratory.

Avoid adjusting a result to match what you expected. Record what you found, then discuss why it may differ.
